# Quasi-MOF-immobilized metal nanoparticles for synergistic catalysis

1AIST-Kyoto University Chemical Energy Materials Open Innovation Laboratory (ChEM-OIL), National Institute of Advanced Industrial Science and Technology (AIST), Yoshida, Sakyo-ku, Kyoto 606-8501, Japan; 2Toyama National College of Technology, 13 Hongo-machi, Toyama 939-8630, Japan; 3School of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou 225002, China
Through partial deligandation of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), quasi-MOFs with a transition structure between MOFs and metal compounds (such as metal oxides, nitrides, sulfides, and phosphides) can be fabricated. Quasi-MOFs can not only retain the porous structure of MOFs to a certain extent, but also expose the inorganic nodes to the guest species (e.g., metal nanoparticles) to show enhanced metal-support interaction for synergistic catalysis. This concept was first demonstrated by our group through calcining Au/MIL-101 at different temperatures under Ar flow to adjust the interface between Au nanoparticles and the inorganic $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ nodes. The obtained Au/quasi-MIL-101 showed superior enhanced catalytic activity in the oxidation of carbon monoxide. This study has inspired further research interest to fabricate other quasi-MOFs through controlled deligandation of mono- and bimetallic MOFs and their composites for the design of efficient catalysts.
## MOFs, metal nanoparticles, metal-support interaction, quasi-MOFs, synergistic catalysis
Citation: Chen L, Tsumori N, Xu Q. Quasi-MOF-immobilized metal nanoparticles for synergistic catalysis. SciChinaChem, 2020, 63, https://doi.org/10.1007/ s11426-020-9781-7
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), constructed by inorganic nodes (metal clusters and/or ions) with organic linkers, have emerged as a promising class of porous materials [1,2]. MOFs have several unique properties, such as high surface area, diverse composition, and easy functionalization, which have provided them with high potential in a variety of fields [3–9], especially in catalysis [10–12]. Moreover, MOFs possess well-defined crystal structures, which are suitable for atomically precise structural characterizations and computational modelling to achieve fundamental understanding of the structure-performance relationship [13]. However, the good crystallinity of MOFs is a double-edged sword because the inorganic nodes are blocked by the organic linkers in the crystalline frameworks, leaving no free positions available for substrate chemisorption, which is not good for their catalytic applications. In some MOFs, the inorganic nodes with coordinated labile ligands (including solvent molecules and modulators) can form coordination unsaturated sites (CUSs) to act as Lewis acid catalysts after the removal of labile ligands by heating or evacuation [14–18]. However, in most cases, the coordination spheres of the inorganic nodes are fully or mostly blocked by the organic linkers, resulting in poor activities of MOFs [19–21].
The removal of organic linkers can expose the inorganic nodes, but it will easily cause the collapse of the framework and a great loss of porosity. Recently, we partially removed the organic linkers to fabricate ‘‘quasi-MOFs’’, which possess a transition-state structure between MOFs and metal compounds (such as metal oxides, nitrides, sulfides, and phosphides) [22]. It demonstrated that quasi-MOFs can not only expose the inorganic nodes, but also retain the porosity of the framework to a certain extent. The exposed inorganic nodes can directly act as catalytically active centers, or cooperate with guest species (e.g., metal nanoparticles (NPs), metal oxides) for synergistic catalysis. The partially retained porous structure can facilitate the diffusion of substrates to reach the active centers and the desorption of products.
Many post-treatment strategies (including thermal transformation, acid/base treatment, solvent assisted ligand exchange, and mechanical treatment) offer the possibilities to partially break the connection between the inorganic nodes and the organic linkers in MOFs [23–27]. Especially, thermal transformation is a powerful strategy to generate a variety of functional materials from MOFs [28–30]. Through careful control of the pyrolysis conditions, such as temperature and gas atmosphere, we have demonstrated the thermal transformation of MOFs for the synthesis of carbon in 2008 [31], metal oxides in 2010 [32], and quasi-MOFs in 2018 [22].
Controlled transformation of MOFs to quasi-MOFs is currently emerging to improve the catalytic performance of mono- and bimetallic MOFs and MOF composites. In this featured article, we review the work of quasi-MOFimmobilized metal NPs for synergistic catalysis and further summarize the significant advances in the development of quasi-MOFs. This featured article is expected to inspire more efforts in the development of quasi-MOFs for a variety of applications.
## 2 Metal/quasi-MOF composites derived from metal/MOF composites for synergistic catalysis
Porous MOFs have shown promising potentials as supports for the immobilization of a variety of guest species, such as metal NPs [33–38]. MOF supports can protect metal NPs from aggregation during catalytic reactions [39–41]. Moreover, the active sites on the MOF backbone can cooperatively work with metal NPs for synergistic catalysis [42,43]. In this regard, functional groups on MOF linkers can interact with metal NPs for synergistic catalysis [44,45]. However, the interaction between inorganic nodes of MOFs and metal NPs is very weak because the presence of organic linkers separates the inorganic nodes from metal NPs. The controlled deligandation of the metal/MOF composites can induce accessible M–O sites to strongly interact with the guest metal NPs while retaining the porosity of the framework to a certain extent. The enhanced interaction between M–O sites and metal NPs can offer an opportunity for sy
nergistic catalysis.
## 2.1 Synthesis of Au/quasi-MIL-101 composites
As a proof of concept, we synthesized composites of Au NPs and a Cr-based MOF (MIL-101) for the controlled deligandation (Figure 1(a)) [22]. Au precursor $\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4})}$ was first impregnated in MIL-101, followed by reduction to Au NPs with aqueous $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ to afford Au/MIL-101 composites. Au/MIL-101 was further thermally treated at various stemperatures (i.e., 373, 473, 573, 673 and 1,073 K) under an inert atmosphere. A variety of characterization techniques were combined to monitor the structural evolution of the MIL-101 framework and interface changes between Au NPs and inorganic $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ nodes at different calcination temperatures.
$\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms showed that Au/MIL-101(373) and Au/MIL-101(473) retained the surface area and pore volume of Au/MIL-101. Au/MIL-101(573) showed a decrease of about $50\%$ in the surface area of Au/MIL-101. Au/MIL-101 (673) lost most of the porosity, indicating the collapse and/or shrinkage of the framework.
Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD), Fourier-transform infrared (FTIR), and Raman measurements showed that $\mathrm{Au}/$ MIL-101(373) and Au/MIL-101(473) retained the crystalline structure of MIL-101. Increasing the calcination temperature to $573\mathrm{K}$ , Au/MIL-101(573) showed partial deligandation of MIL-101 with incipient formation of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . The partial deligandation of MIL-101 led to strong interactions between the inorganic $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ nodes and Au NPs, which was reflected by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The $\mathrm{Cr2p}$ and $\mathrm{Au}4\mathrm{f}$ peaks for Au/MIL-101(573) both shifted to a higher binding energy than those for $\mathrm{Au}/$ MIL-101, implying a modification of electronic structures of $\mathrm{Cr}$ and Au by deligandation of MIL-101 and in turn strong interactions between $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ nodes and Au NPs. For Au/MIL101(673), a greater extent of deligandation of MIL-101 with more $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ formed was observed.
Combined with thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) results, we calculated the weight loss at each calcination temperature to illustrate the structural changes of MIL-101 under thermal treatment. The MIL-101 support underwent release of guest $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ at $373\mathrm{K}$ , followed by removal of coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{{OH/F}}$ groups on the $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ nodes at $473\mathrm{K}$ , consequent release of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from the terephthalate ligand to form quasiMIL-101 at $573\mathrm{K}$ , and finally collapse of the framework along with carbon formation above $673\mathrm{K}$ (Figure 1(b)).
## 2.2 Synergistic catalysis of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{u}/$ quasi-MIL-101 in CO oxidation
In the CO oxidation, the calcined Au/MIL-101 materials showed enhanced activity compared with the non-calcined $\mathrm{Au/MIL}{\cdot}101$ (Figure 1(c)). With the increase of calcination temperatures from 373 to $573\mathrm{K}$ , the catalytic activity increased. Further increasing the calcination temperature above $673\mathrm{K}$ resulted in a remarkable decrease in activity. Au/MIL-101(573) (Au/quasi-MIL-101) exhibited the highest activity, maintaining $100\%$ CO conversion up to $1{,}700\mathrm{min}$ . The exhibited superior catalytic performance of Au/quasi-MIL-101 was attributed to the strong metalsupport interaction and the porous structure of quasi-MIL101. The close contact of $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ with Au NPs allowed them to work cooperatively for synergistic catalysis in CO oxidation. In the catalytic system, the accessible $\mathrm{Cr-O}$ sites in quasiMIL-101 could uptake and activate $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ molecules and release active oxygen species to the CO molecules adsorbed on the Au NPs. Moreover, the quasi-MOF provided a porous structure to benefit the diffusion of substrates to access to the active sites, thus resulting in high catalytic performance.
## 2.3 Metal/quasi-MOF composites derived from other metal/MOF composites
The partial thermal transformation strategy has been successfully extended to other metal/MOF composites for the synthesis of metal/quasi-MOF composites [46,47]. It is worth mentioning that the calcination atmosphere has a great effect on the catalytic efficiency of the calcined materials. In this regard, Zhou and co-workers [46] calcined M/CeBTC $\scriptstyle({\mathrm{M}}={\mathrm{Au}}$ , Cu or Au-Cu, BTC $\varprojlim\varprojlim2$ 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate) under different atmospheres (air, $\Nu_{2}$ , and $4\%\mathrm{O}_{2},$ ) to tune the interactions between metal NPs and $\mathrm{Ce-O}$ nodes for CO oxidation (Figure 2(a)). M/CeBTC calcined at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air (M/CeBTC-300A) resulted in complete decomposition of Ce-BTC to $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ . M/CeBTC calcined at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\Nu_{2}$ (M/ CeBTC-500N) contained $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and quasi Ce-BTC phase. Further thermal treatments of $\mathrm{M}/\mathrm{CeBTC}{-}500\mathrm{N}$ in dilute oxygen $(4\%\mathrm{~O~}_{2})$ atmosphere $(\mathrm{Au\mathrm{-}C u/C e B T C\mathrm{-}500N\mathrm{-}270D})$ could selectively remove carbon generated from deligandation in quasi Ce-BTC. Au-Cu/CeBTC-500N-270D with “clean” catalyst surface showed a strong interaction between the inorganic nodes and metal NPs, as demonstrated by temperature-programmed reduction (TPR) profiles. The peak related to surface $\mathrm{Ce}^{4+}$ reduction shifted from $770^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in Ce-BTC-A to $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in $\mathrm{Au{-}C u/C e B T C{-}{500N{-}270D}}$ , implying the strong interaction between the Ce–O nodes and Au-Cu NPs in $\mathrm{Au{-}C u/C e B T C{-}}500\mathrm{N}{-}270\mathrm{D}$ . In the oxidation of CO, M/CeBTC exhibited inferior activity. Thermal treatment of M/CeBTC greatly enhanced the catalytic activity (Figure 2(b)). $T_{100}$ (temperature to reach $100\%$ of CO conversion) of Au/CeBTC-300A, Au-Cu/CeBTC-500N, and $\mathrm{Au{-}C u/C e B T C{-}}500\mathrm{N{-}270D}$ was 100, 250, and $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. Au/CeBTC-300A showed an inferior activity due to a great loss of porous structure. Au/CeBTC-500N exhibited poor performance owing to the blockage of active sites by the carbon generated from partial deligandation. These results demonstrated that both the porous structure and strong interactions between Ce-nodes and Au-Cu NPs were essential for $\mathrm{Au\mathrm{-}C u/C e B T C\mathrm{-}500N\mathrm{-}270D}$ to achieve the high activity. Moreover, $\mathrm{Au-Cu/CeBTC-500N-270D}$ maintained $100\%$ CO conversion at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $100\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 2(c)). The high stability was contributed to both the strong interaction of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ with metal NPs and the confinement effect of the quasi Ce-BTC.
## 3 Metal/quasi-MOF composites derived from pristine MOFs for catalysis
The partial transformation strategy can be extended to pristine MOFs for the synthesis of metal NPs/oxides immobilized in quasi-MOFs. During the transformation, some of the metal–organic bonds break to form metal clusters, while the others are preserved to form quasi-MOFs with distorted structures. The as-formed metal clusters can act as active catalysts in the reaction, and the quasi-MOFs provide a porous structure for the diffusion of substrates and products and prevent the aggregation of metal clusters during the reaction. In this regard, mono- and bimetallic MOFs have been employed as precursors for the synthesis of quasi-MOF immobilized metal NP/oxide composites.
## 3.1 Metal/quasi-MOF composites derived from monometallic MOFs
The controlled thermal transformation method has been applied to monometallic MOFs for the synthesis of quasiMOF-immobilized metal NPs, showing high performance in organocatalysis and electrocatalysis [48,49].
Han and co-workers [48] prepared quasi-MIL-101 with high concentration of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ molecular clusters for dehydrofluorination of 1,1,1,3,3-pentafluoropropane (HFC245fa). MIL-101 was calcined in $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere at temperatures between 350 and $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to afford quasi-MIL-101 encapsulating $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ molecular clusters (Figure 3(a)). MIL101 calcined at 350, 400, 450, $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ contained $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ particles with sizes of 0.47, 0.55, 0.6, and $1.67\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. According to PXRD patterns, the diffraction peaks around $5^{\circ}-10^{\circ}$ for MIL-101 showed more and more broadening with the increase of calcination temperature from 350 to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and no characteristic peaks of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ were detected for all calcined samples. The specific surface area dropped significantly upon calcination, from $2{,}965\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (MIL-101) to $415\mathrm{m}^{2}/\dot{\mathrm{g}}$ (MIL-101-350) and $236.6\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (MIL-101-500). In the dehydrofluorination of HFC-245fa to 1,3,3,3-tetrafluoropropene (HFO-1234ze), the activity of quasi-MIL-101 materials first increased with increasing the calcination temperature from 350 to $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , then decreased with further increasing the calcination temperature to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 3 (b)). MIL-101-450 achieved the highest activity, with a reaction rate of about $4.5\:\mathrm{mmol/(h\g)}$ . The inferior activities of MIL-101-350 and MIL-101-400 were due to their less exposed $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , while the low activity of MIL-101-500 was caused by the full decomposition of MIL-101 structure with significant sintering of $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . For comparison, commercial $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ exhibited an initial activity of $3\mathrm{mmol/(h}\mathrm{g})$ , but dropped to less than $1\ \mathrm{mmol/(h\g)}$ after $20\mathrm{h}$ . MIL-101-450 exhibited stable activity, attributing to the small $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ clusters confined in quasi-MIL-101 to inhibit the formation of coke (Figure 3(c)).
Pang and co-workers [49] fabricated a series of quasi-ZIF67 materials from calcination of ZIF-67 for electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER). The nanostructure change of ZIF-67 after calcination was revealed by spherical aberration-corrected transmission electron microscopy (TEM). Both facets (002) and (011) could be clearly observed for pristine ZIF-67 crystal, while only the surface (002) could be seen for quasi-ZIF-67-350. Moreover, ultrafine Co clusters were observed in quasi-ZIF-67-350. Therefore, different from pristine ZIF-67 with good crystallinity, quasi-ZIF-67- 350 displayed exposed metal sites with reduced crystallinity. All calcined samples displayed improved OER performances compared with that of pristine ZIF-67. Among all the calcined samples, quasi-ZIF-67-350 displayed the best OER performance, with an overpotential of $286\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ in $1.0\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}$ . The exhibited highest electrocatalytic activity of quasi-ZIF-67-350 could be attributed to its large surface area of $2038.2\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and lowest charge transfer resistance of $15.0\Omega$ .
## 3.2 Metal/quasi-MOF composites derived from bimetallic MOFs
The controlled thermal transformation method has been successfully applied to bimetallic MOFs for the synthesis of quasi-MOF-immobilized metal NPs [50,51]. In bimetallic MOFs, the two metal ions have different coordination abilities with the organic linkers. The metal ions with weaker coordination with the organic linkers transform to metal $\mathrm{NPs}/$ compounds under certain conditions while the other metal ions remain coordinating with the organic linkers to partially retain the structure of the framework.
Hu and co-workers [51] reported the synthesis of a Cunanowire@quasi-MOF composite through partial thermal decomposition of a benzimidazole-modified $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetalMOF. PXRD of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetal-MOF calcined at different temperatures revealed that metal $\mathrm{cu}$ was formed at $280^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the crystal structure of MOFs was partially retained below $360^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 4(a)). $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetal-MOF was calcined at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ under $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere to afford Cu-nanowire $@$ quasi-MOF. TEM images demonstrated that Cu-nanowire $@$ quasi-MOF maintained the octahedral morphology of $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ bimetal-MOF, with curly nanowires embedded in the quasi-MOF (Figure 4(b)). $\Nu_{2}$ adsorptiondesorption isotherms of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetal-MOF and $\mathrm{Cu}$ -nanowire $@$ quasi-MOF displayed a decrease of surface area with generation of mesopores after thermal treatment. Under the similar calcination condition, monometallic Cu-MOF transformed to core-shell $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}(\ensuremath{\mathcal{Q}}\mathrm{N}$ -doped carbon composites (Figure 4(c)). During the calcination process, Cu-ligand bonds with weaker coordination would break and form metastable $\mathrm{CuNPs}$ , while the Co-MOF part with higher stability would serve as a morphology retainer and a nano-channel template to reassemble the Cu NPs into $\mathrm{Cu}$ -nanowires (Figure 4(d)). In the selective oxidation of benzyl alcohol (Figure 4(e, f)), $\mathrm{Cu}$ -nanowire $@$ quasi-MOF $[\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetalMOF(300)] exhibited $62.8\%$ conversion of benzyl alcohol with $98.8\%$ selectivity to benzaldehyde, superior to those of $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ bimetal-MOF(260), $\mathrm{Cu/Co}$ bimetal-MOF(280) and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Co}$ bimetal-MOF(340). The enhanced activity of Cunanowire $@$ quasi-MOF could be attributed to the hierarchical porous structure of quasi-MOF for mass transfer and catalytically active Cu-nanowires with a high surface area for intense contacts with the reactants.
## 4 Conclusions and perspective
Quasi-MOFs, fabricated through partial deligandation of MOFs, can both expose the inorganic nodes and keep the porous structure. Quasi-MOFs show a transition structure between MOFs and metal compounds, which can bridge the gap between MOFs and metal compounds. Partial deligandation of MOF composites can enhance the interaction between the immobilized guest species and the inorganic nodes, allowing them to show synergistic effect for efficient catalysis. Partial deligandation of pristine MOFs can afford catalytically active metal/metal oxide clusters confined within quasi-MOFs. The controlled transformation of MOFs and MOF composites to quasi-MOF composites can open new avenues for the design of efficient catalysts. To evaluate the catalytic efficiency of the resulted quasi-MOF composites, it is important to calculate turnover number (TON) and turnover frequency (TOF) of the catalysts. The investigation of quasi-MOFs is still in its infancy, and many unique features of quasi-MOFs remain to be explored. For the future research on quasi-MOFs, here we present some personal perspectives for reference: (1) The exploration of other deligandation methods for the synthesis of quasi-MOFs is expected. Up to now, quasi-MOFs are usually obtained through partial thermal transformation. Other post-treatment strategies, especially liquid-phase methods, which may offer more opportunities to delicately control the transformation process, should be developed for the synthesis of quasi-MOFs. (2) The number of quasi-MOFs needs to be expanded. More types of MOFs are desired to be explored as precursors for the synthesis of quasi-MOFs. (3) The distorted structures of quasi-MOFs and the transformation process of MOFs to quasi-MOFs still lack detailed characterizations and investigations. Advanced characterization techniques should be combined for a better understanding of the molecular structures of quasi-MOFs. In situ/operando characterization techniques can be employed to provide fundamental information about the structure transformation. (4) The unique properties of quasi-MOFs remain to be explored. Different from good crystallization of MOFs, the distorted structures of quasi-MOFs may impart unique physical and chemical properties. (5) The applications of quasi-MOFs in other fields are to be found. In addition to catalysis, quasi-MOFs with exposed inorganic nodes and porous structures may also have high potentials in gas adsorption and electrochemical storage and conversion.
Controlled deligandation of MOFs for the fabrication of quasi-MOFs will pave the way to the development of nextgeneration functional materials. Continued research effort in this exciting area can be expected to enable applications of quasi-MOFs in various fields.
